  "structure": {
    "block_count": 49,
    "blocks": [
      {
        "block_id": "block_000000",
        "block_index": 0,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h1",
        "text": "Crawling Safety Tips",
        "text_sha256": "c6eb55c33019b5545a13aa1a9878a4487ace91304e42e1d36a7a3b8a4e3c2ca4",
        "word_count": 3
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000001",
        "block_index": 1,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "by Heidi Murkoff , author of What to Expect When You're Expecting.",
        "text_sha256": "fe0f379871761f84993fc97638e1be60dfaee98ef922ce30fb241d275bfed786",
        "word_count": 12
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000002",
        "block_index": 2,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Ask Heidi Murkoff",
        "text_sha256": "ac7d5d65ef8df9ec0a9d8053a467eff02d16dae53d1cbcba802e080bfc2dd938",
        "word_count": 3
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000003",
        "block_index": 3,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "\"My daughter has just started crawling. Are there any crawling safety tips I should keep in mind?\"",
        "text_sha256": "b0f0bb10317d45b32ba2f296a0105fa956975db692c14db9d64bc91969793b5c",
        "word_count": 17
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000004",
        "block_index": 4,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Congratulations — and welcome to a whole new phase of babyhood! Now that your crawling infant has mastered movement on all fours, she's able to explore her world, pique her curiosity and engage in some skill-boosting activities (like scooting toward the ball instead of crying for you to bring it to her).",
        "text_sha256": "cf9588fec2f847db2637fe4a5081b028a5ad0f45553b7b1ff0e264adc8f1c8f8",
        "word_count": 52
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000005",
        "block_index": 5,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "But along with your baby’s newfound mobility comes the potential for mischief as she crawls toward trouble spots like electrical outlets or the cat’s litter box. So it’s time to start thinking about crawling safety. These tips will help ensure that your little mover and shaker segues into crawling with as few bumps and bruises as possible.",
        "text_sha256": "993e24646badcc2c726e0b7470393029a59f0836248ec60c28401af46542df44",
        "word_count": 57
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000006",
        "block_index": 6,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Do some more babyproofing",
        "text_sha256": "698890a5f89be4e9852d585318cf4ee447ddb227febeb8b136148816326c07d1",
        "word_count": 4
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000007",
        "block_index": 7,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Haven't gotten around to making your home a safe place for baby? Now is the time to get serious about babyproofing . Here are some crawling safety basics:",
        "text_sha256": "a25eff1c162cce40c67dfbcb209546239001d6ad4cdacad0ce7512aab8276340",
        "word_count": 28
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000008",
        "block_index": 8,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Move electrical and window cords, which are strangulation hazards, out of the way.",
        "text_sha256": "bb7efb872c4220a5853abeb441219efc3a88efd0ed1a047c5c855dfd456ff47a",
        "word_count": 13
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000009",
        "block_index": 9,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Plug up outlets. You can even replace the switchplates with ones that automatically slide to cover up outlets when they’re not in use.",
        "text_sha256": "2ed1b080e41db2b7bf30fc5cb2b6a815755c9d0cd1306016f448e15f151028aa",
        "word_count": 23
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000010",
        "block_index": 10,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Lock cabinets, especially ones containing dangerous items like poisons, medicines, sharp objects or breakables.",
        "text_sha256": "c58b0be9cc8243a8b48c8f357e65961ef53ffa9c3f6e5c111222eddece80fe02",
        "word_count": 14
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000011",
        "block_index": 11,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Prevent choking. Get rid of items on the floor that are small enough to fit into your baby's mouth, like pet food. Your baby will likely mouth anything she gets her hands on!",
        "text_sha256": "ff3c64ca92fe988a00282bf3a5e72bb6c199faa154cd241a075ae70e6240cfd3",
        "word_count": 33
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000012",
        "block_index": 12,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Section off danger zones . Put safety gates in front of areas like the bathroom or keep doors closed. If you have a pool, install a gated fence around it.",
        "text_sha256": "62d2c2be91a887f5ceb229fc753c203f53ebc5f12631f7b23f6e61333225af59",
        "word_count": 30
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000013",
        "block_index": 13,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Block off stairs. If your house has multiple levels, you’ll need to install a gate at the top and bottom of the stairs. That way your baby won’t be able to crawl up from the bottom and tumble back down. (Though you may want to put the bottom gate a few steps up from the landing so that your baby has a chance to practice her crawling skills on the bottom steps.)",
        "text_sha256": "292f44208fe2c3a448e7b7882e1ca3138aca899e414c5b55196107a8fb7e8538",
        "word_count": 72
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000014",
        "block_index": 14,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Add bumpers to sharp furniture edges.",
        "text_sha256": "e0ee63d58639bba5ac8ee09d5620b5ef76961e6ed865e3cfa69955218f9871ca",
        "word_count": 6
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000015",
        "block_index": 15,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Secure heavy furniture to the walls, including dressers and stoves.",
        "text_sha256": "ae2eb3ece15537d7718ccd29444b87702fe2454597b5d1251afb461fda0db30f",
        "word_count": 10
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000016",
        "block_index": 16,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Try crawling yourself",
        "text_sha256": "e44b3abc46ec31136d1cf7a08d3869cf36d1d81e3a5f8196ca4af675e4947327",
        "word_count": 3
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000017",
        "block_index": 17,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "A great way to help you understand the view your child has of the world — and see what potential dangers she may encounter (even if you have babyproofed) — is to get down on the ground and crawl with her. You may notice a long-forgotten possible choking hazard under the armchair or discover that the edge of your coffee table is sharper than you thought!",
        "text_sha256": "a989159f2f03d04553f6f37de9c5c3a9b4323a732ba3f8fcfe365c568c59afad",
        "word_count": 66
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000018",
        "block_index": 18,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Crawling around with your baby will also give you the chance to teach her some crawling safety tips — i.e. what’s okay for her to grab and what’s not. It may take a while for her to catch on to the fact that she can’t have the food in the dog’s bowl but she can play with the Tupperware in the kitchen cabinet. Just keep at it though — after enough repetition on your part, she’ll get the picture.",
        "text_sha256": "0b4b64099035eb1575e7c7e86aebd31f58bc4269e117feeb135304bccb3d4274",
        "word_count": 79
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000019",
        "block_index": 19,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Prep your hardwood floors for crawling",
        "text_sha256": "31d4262de6e61f211216a0de35eed0fde63761de08a3225871414eeed937cdce",
        "word_count": 6
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000020",
        "block_index": 20,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Make sure that wood floors are free from splinters, nails or any other sharp objects so that your baby has a smooth surface on which to roam. If you don’t have many carpeted areas in your home, you may want to consider getting a non-slip rug or floor padding to give your baby the chance to crawl on some soft surfaces — and reduce any bruising to her tender knees. You can find colorful floor pads made especially for little ones at baby stores.",
        "text_sha256": "19409d537ba606630395b25abbe5f845ae9df831c1bcc1df37f7e5063e35d7fa",
        "word_count": 84
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000021",
        "block_index": 21,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Dress your baby in pants (or crawling knee pads)",
        "text_sha256": "6ae4cc50b346988445b5b05bed8641f542f4052ee2d284c6d340581b22e6be09",
        "word_count": 9
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000022",
        "block_index": 22,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "You may love the way your little fashionista looks in dresses and skirts, but while she’s starting to crawl , these garments could slow her down and frustrate her. But style doesn’t have to take a backseat to crawling safety. Simply don your baby in comfy pants (the better to cushion her knees) or lightweight leggings in warmer weather. Got a baby boy? Make sure his pants aren’t too baggy or they’ll bunch up at the knees and interfere with his efforts.",
        "text_sha256": "4bf41f0898cc5265253891da4ee577450687c005e757af7605caade2384ba49c",
        "word_count": 82
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000023",
        "block_index": 23,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "If you really feel the need to protect your little one’s legs when sporting shorts, there are baby crawling knee pads you can buy.",
        "text_sha256": "aad702fb3d6eacd008a847dccd5c00ecaf23f2f7e8db89504ca4042b9b5379a5",
        "word_count": 24
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000024",
        "block_index": 24,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Maintain an eagle eye",
        "text_sha256": "0c74babb8126dd592922fb9b331a2e5566f6a928d0aabd84d91142d34bc2d579",
        "word_count": 4
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000025",
        "block_index": 25,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "No matter what crawling safety precautions you’ve taken in your home, you’ll still need to watch your newly minted crawler at all times to make sure she doesn’t get into any mischief or get hurt. The only time you can safely take your eyes off your baby is when she’s in her crib or play yard.",
        "text_sha256": "527c8546530a2ba0cc12c1c7a7f0d08e80f8e94534ca5c8e42de743319ef913f",
        "word_count": 56
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000026",
        "block_index": 26,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "And remember that when you’re not at home, your little crawler will be extra interested in exploring the new environment, which means you'll need to be extra vigilant about keeping tabs on her!",
        "text_sha256": "b7b68ab4f0aa0bd2d6704132439f0f5f52545ea23fb71c0f5b36285e82af805e",
        "word_count": 33
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000027",
        "block_index": 27,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Happy (and safe) exploring!",
        "text_sha256": "97de840f344dd3f3fe56f419335736019cac3d1511e106cf4610967be5898e0d",
        "word_count": 4
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000028",
        "block_index": 28,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Heidi Murkoff",
        "text_sha256": "6f05d1b1394d028842497499fff96bffb14810fb4a10d1a23a1ef1b82a72292e",
        "word_count": 2
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000029",
        "block_index": 29,
        "block_type": "blockquote",
        "tag": "blockquote",
        "text": "What to Expect the First Year , 3rd edition, Heidi Murkoff. WhatToExpect.com, Babyproofing the House: A Guide to Keeping Your Child Safe , January 2021. WhatToExpect.com, When Do Babies Start Crawling? , June 2021. WhatToExpect.com, Why Babies Mouth Everything , January 2019. Nemours, Growing, Moving, Learning , May 2011. American Academy of Pediatrics, Crawling Styles , January 2013. American Academy of Pediatrics, Movement Milestones: Babies 4 to 7 Months , March 2021. American Academy of Pediatrics, Childproofing Your Home for Poisons , February 2021.",
        "text_sha256": "9f9b96cacdd635bd9c79a2f2fa071f259de8f00da196e4567bd16f0f0bbcde34",
        "word_count": 84
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000030",
        "block_index": 30,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Go to Your Baby's Age",
        "text_sha256": "05a2c9b32af919d9a0a87b2762079ec33dc5246c16eaee9f0c85c8e3a83fe2ad",
        "word_count": 5
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000031",
        "block_index": 31,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Your Next Read",
        "text_sha256": "6d2b65f6bc74d3f3d5656d8c88c806ff70605a0ce9d4fd990efe9af093220755",
        "word_count": 3
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000032",
        "block_index": 32,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "When Do Babies Start Crawling? Medically Reviewed by Lauren Crosby, M.D., F.A.A.P.",
        "text_sha256": "49e7b50e6e097ef5d739341779b6a0929056066fb37a1d10cad40f117de4d4ac",
        "word_count": 12
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000033",
        "block_index": 33,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "8 Walking Safety Tips for Toddlers to Keep Spills and Hazards at Bay Fact Checked by Christine Gordon",
        "text_sha256": "086ec9d5de7ebda411d019f0d5e9930eab8c42d7068c8029ad58783018700917",
        "word_count": 18
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000034",
        "block_index": 34,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "How to Babyproof Every Room in the House Medically Reviewed by Gina Posner, M.D., F.A.A.P.",
        "text_sha256": "f6c5d52e5236cb2a03d03eb040a86c872746c299ac20be6c1c9ea477d662b97b",
        "word_count": 15
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000035",
        "block_index": 35,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "When Do Babies Start Walking? Medically Reviewed by Micah Resnick, M.D., F.A.A.P.",
        "text_sha256": "d3117a6d434c2db55ef9d073dc03e547921f827f5fd7f7110d6f120aaaeaaf83",
        "word_count": 12
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000036",
        "block_index": 36,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "How to Play With Your Baby Safely Medically Reviewed by Jesil Pazhayampallil, M.D., F.A.A.P.",
        "text_sha256": "3132149b1adc4c24fcc3f161acebb7d8b75cd984883c938126d514998024940e",
        "word_count": 14
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000037",
        "block_index": 37,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "How to Make Sure Your Baby's Crib Is Safe Medically Reviewed by Gina Posner, M.D., F.A.A.P.",
        "text_sha256": "ad3a0c8f03c263503395757f245dae0d59f567784a826a0f47c46e902fb7937c",
        "word_count": 16
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000038",
        "block_index": 38,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Why Toddlers Love Climbing and How to Keep Them Safe Medically Reviewed by Kyle Monk, M.D.",
        "text_sha256": "7a3d4cf76eb04b2d32f792e24d295dcfa5f87b3b0da72f5850af575c9cc88207",
        "word_count": 16
